<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>After O5-2 doesn’t report to several meetings, the O5 Council begins an investigation. They find O5-2 easily, as an anomalous event in Oregon is investigated, finding a deceased female matching the description of O5-2. Her body is taken in, with no evidence as to why she was there or how she died.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>How could this be allowed to happen? The O5 council being as important as they are, having on just up and vanish seems like it would set off a lot of red alerts before getting to this point.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>While Eurydice is paranoid for obvious reasons, she lets her guard down with Orpheus, thinking he will protect her.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Protect her more than all the MTF and security officers the Foundation has at their disposal? There is letting your guard down, and there is just asking for trouble. This seems like the latter.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>In melancholy, he attempts to resurrect his love. He asks SCP-343 “how to resurrect Eurydice,” in which SCP-343 tells him that “it requires Orpheus to convince the Arch-Drudgeon,” something SCP-343 doesn’t want to get involved with. Orpheus establishes a plan and first enters The Wanderer’s Library to find “The Song of Minor,” fighting several SH members and subduing an entity known as Cerberus to obtain the lyrics to the song.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>How does he get to SCP-343? If this dude is an SH member, how is he getting to an SCP the Foundation has in custody? It's not like he can just waltz in the front door of Site-19.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>He arrives to Site 0 and uses a verse to please SCP-001, with the anomaly leading Orpheus through the gate and directly to JALAKARA’s doorstep.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>While I'm pretty sure you mean the Gate Guardian, please keep in mind we have so, so many SCP-001s. You need to be more specific.</p>
<p>While the focus here is a lot more narrow than the previous version of the idea you offered, it does still feel quite clustered for what essentially boils down to a retelling of a fairly well known Greek myth.</p>
<p>Even if you narrow the scope down further, I'm not too sure how the reception for such a close 1:1 transfer of a well-known story into the SCP mythos would be.</p>
<p>I think you still need more refinement on this idea, and repair of the current plot holes as mentioned above before I would be fine offering the greenlight.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Best of luck.</p>

							</div>

								
					
